{"name":"Bleach White","hex":"#ebe1ce","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bleach","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A chemical, such as sodium hypochlorite or hydrogen peroxide, or a preparation of such a chemical, used for disinfecting or whitening.","A variety of bleach."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To treat with bleach, especially so as to whiten (fabric, paper, etc.) or lighten (hair).","To be whitened or lightened (by the sun, for example)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bleach"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bleach","blocks":[{"text":"Bleach is the generic name for any chemical product that is used industrially or domestically to remove color from fabric, fiber, or food or to disinfect after cleaning. It often refers specifically to a dilute solution of sodium hypochlorite, also called \"liquid bleach\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Clorox_Bleach_products.jpg/330px-Clorox_Bleach_products.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bleach","wikidata":"Q11587"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Old_method_of_Bleaching_Figure_20_Appleton_1884.tif/lossless-page1-500px-Old_method_of_Bleaching_Figure_20_Appleton_1884.tif.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Old_method_of_Bleaching_Figure_20_Appleton_1884.tif/lossless-page1-1280px-Old_method_of_Bleaching_Figure_20_Appleton_1884.tif.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Old_method_of_Bleaching_Figure_20_Appleton_1884.tif/lossless-page1-500px-Old_method_of_Bleaching_Figure_20_Appleton_1884.tif.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Early method of bleaching cotton and linen goods on lawns, using a combination of exposure to direct sunlight and the application of water","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOld_method_of_Bleaching_Figure_20_Appleton_1884.tif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Canonization_2014-The_Canonization_of_Saint_John_XXIII_and_Saint_John_Paul_II_%2814037015405%29.jpg/500px-Canonization_2014-The_Canonization_of_Saint_John_XXIII_and_Saint_John_Paul_II_%2814037015405%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Canonization_2014-The_Canonization_of_Saint_John_XXIII_and_Saint_John_Paul_II_%2814037015405%29.jpg/1280px-Canonization_2014-The_Canonization_of_Saint_John_XXIII_and_Saint_John_Paul_II_%2814037015405